                    "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
                    "body": ">   Current buffer setting can also be set for the particular file type via `after/ftplugin/<filetype>.lua` with `vim.bo.commentstring = '-- %s'`.\r\n\r\nYeah, I am afraid adding this type of disclaimers opens a door to many more cases like that. There needs to be a hard cutoff in what knowledge is assumed readers of help page already have.\r\n\r\nI think having the 'commentstring' link to an option (which is assumed people know that this is a navigatable with `<C-]>` link) which says that this is a \"local to buffer\" option should be enough to convey all possible ways for users to interact with it.\r\n\r\nThe 'after/ftplugin/\\<filetype>.lua' is a way to create a `:h ftplugin` which is a common way to set buffer-local options based on its filetype (i.e. \"language\", more or less). Another way is to create an autocommand for `:h FileType` event, but I prefer ftplugin approach.",
                    "bodyHTML": "<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Current buffer setting can also be set for the particular file type via <code class=\"notranslate\">after/ftplugin/&lt;filetype&gt;.lua</code> with <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.bo.commentstring = '-- %s'</code>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Yeah, I am afraid adding this type of disclaimers opens a door to many more cases like that. There needs to be a hard cutoff in what knowledge is assumed readers of help page already have.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">I think having the 'commentstring' link to an option (which is assumed people know that this is a navigatable with <code class=\"notranslate\">&lt;C-]&gt;</code> link) which says that this is a \"local to buffer\" option should be enough to convey all possible ways for users to interact with it.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">The 'after/ftplugin/&lt;filetype&gt;.lua' is a way to create a <code class=\"notranslate\">:h ftplugin</code> which is a common way to set buffer-local options based on its filetype (i.e. \"language\", more or less). Another way is to create an autocommand for <code class=\"notranslate\">:h FileType</code> event, but I prefer ftplugin approach.</p>",
                    "bodyText": "Current buffer setting can also be set for the particular file type via after/ftplugin/<filetype>.lua with vim.bo.commentstring = '-- %s'.\n\nYeah, I am afraid adding this type of disclaimers opens a door to many more cases like that. There needs to be a hard cutoff in what knowledge is assumed readers of help page already have.\nI think having the 'commentstring' link to an option (which is assumed people know that this is a navigatable with <C-]> link) which says that this is a \"local to buffer\" option should be enough to convey all possible ways for users to interact with it.\nThe 'after/ftplugin/<filetype>.lua' is a way to create a :h ftplugin which is a common way to set buffer-local options based on its filetype (i.e. \"language\", more or less). Another way is to create an autocommand for :h FileType event, but I prefer ftplugin approach.",
